{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1165886-london-setting-inspiration","id":1165886,"title":"London Setting Inspiration","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-12-07T09:18:20-08:00","id":1165886,"title":"London Setting Inspiration","content":"I have often used London (UK not Ontario!) as a setting and have used various works of urban fantasy fiction as inspiration especially Neil Gaiman's Neverwhere, Ben Aaronovich's Rivers of London series, Mike Shevdon's Court's of the Feyre series, and more recently Tom Pollock's Skyscraper Throne series. I've come across other series like Benedict Jacka's Alex Versus series and Peter Mcleans' Burned Man series and read one book from the series but found them not to add much to London as a setting. Looking to add to the background of London as a setting and wondering what other urban fantasy or other genre literature can be used for inspiration for the supernatural aspects of London. Thanks!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12792-scifirabbi","name":"scifirabbi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12792_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"65"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-12-07T10:30:33-08:00","id":1165894,"title":"","content":"It's not a book recommendation but it's worth remembering that London has a lot of surveillance cameras, many supernaturals will need a way to counter that and I imagine that any organised supernaturals would be willing to work together to keep themselves safe.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4714-live-bait","name":"Live Bait","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"703"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-12-09T14:24:59-08:00","id":1166338,"title":"","content":"Most of Hellblazer stories are set on London, as are actually many in the DC Vertigo imprint. You'll see a lot of Neil Gaiman's stuff there, by the way. The tone and theme of those comics usually mesh well with CoD.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-12-09T14:34:45-08:00","id":1166340,"title":"","content":"London Falling and sequels are good. Bitter Seeds and the Laundry Files are both urban fantasy meets spy novels. The sadly short lived BBC Drama The Fades is another recommendation. And honestly, you cannot go wrong with Doctor Who. It's not urban fantasy but the big name British urban fantasy writers like Neil Gaiman and Ben Aaronovich have written for Doctor who so it's a big influence. The UK version of Being Human is an absolute must.<br /> <br /> I get the feeling that UK urban fantasy puts far more emphasis on the interaction between the supernatural and the mundane than the supernatural does. Non-Hunter Mortals come up way more often, and have way more plot relevance, in Rivers of London than the Dresden Files. And that's before you get to the fact that perfectly mundane police procedures are omnipresent in Peter Grant's life. So that's something I'd focus on, and something I used very heavily when writing the Office of the Lord Stewards (it's in Dream Catchers, see my sig).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>\u201cThere are no rules. Only Principles and natural laws.\u201d - Promethius<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/17686-the-kings-raven-s-homebrwe-index\" target=\"_blank\">My Homebrew no longer fits in a signature, you can find an index of it here</a>.<br /> <b>Full length fan-books I contributed too: </b><a href=\"https://sites.google.com/site/princessthehopeful/files/PrincessTheHopeful.pdf?attredirects=0&amp;d=1\" target=\"_blank\">Princess: the Hopeful</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/0B9DMG5y7ZdngekVaMGNQbTF4RzA\" target=\"_blank\">Leviathan: the Tempest</a>, <a href=\"https://sites.google.com/site/thekingsraven/files/DreamCatchers.pdf?attredirects=0&amp;d=1\" target=\"_blank\">Dream Catchers</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/134-the-kings-raven","name":"The Kings Raven","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar134_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3003"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-12-10T09:09:29-08:00","id":1166475,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Kings Raven</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1165886-london-setting-inspiration?p=1166340#post1166340\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The UK version of Being Human is an absolute must.</div> </div> </div> </div>\u200bNote that Being Human is not set in London so may not meet the OP's needs.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>- Chris Allen, Aberrant Line Developer, Freelance Writer<br /> <br /> \u200bLike my work? Feel like helping me stay supplied with tea? Check out my <b><a href=\"https://www.patreon.com/acrozatarim\" target=\"_blank\">Patreon</a></b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1757-acrozatarim","name":"Acrozatarim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1757_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1892"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-12-10T09:32:46-08:00","id":1166477,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Kings Raven</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1165886-london-setting-inspiration?p=1166340#post1166340\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I get the feeling that UK urban fantasy puts far more emphasis on the interaction between the supernatural and the mundane than the supernatural does.</div> </div> </div> </div>I have the same impression, and I love this approach. It reminds me of the only supernatural american show that I really like, more on some seasons than others, Grimm. Though it isn't in London at all, I guess that the mood of many episodes can help.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-12-10T11:51:52-08:00","id":1166509,"title":"","content":"The first volume of Grant Morrison's Invisibles, for the part withDane McGowan/Jack Frost's period in London with Tom O'Bedlam. <br /> <br /> I'd mention Simon R. Green's Nightside series, but it may be too &quot;out there&quot; and over the top for what you want. <br /> <br /> Ben Aaronovitch's Rivers of London series, maybe. <br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-12-10T12:22:23-08:00","id":1166513,"title":"","content":"I believe the is a 1e source book Shadows of the UK. It focuses mostly on Werewolf but has sections on the other splats. Is that what you have been using? If not I recommend it. It would need conversion to 2e but has tons of ideas.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13691-margul","name":"Margul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"50"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-12-10T13:05:06-08:00","id":1166519,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>scifirabbi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1165886-london-setting-inspiration#post1165886\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Looking to add to the background of London as a setting and wondering what other urban fantasy or other genre literature can be used for inspiration for the supernatural aspects of London. Thanks!</div> </div> </div> </div>A good book on the history of London would probably help a lot. There's bound to be a few things in there that would pique your interest and let you build from there.<br /> <br /> This website is pretty clickbaity but has a lot of little interesting things, like the subway only used to deliver mail, or the Temple of Mithras, or <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tipu%27s_Tiger\" target=\"_blank\">Tipu's Tiger.</a> Not supernatural on their own, but could easily be linked to other things.<br /> <br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"><img src=\"https://vanda-production-assets.s3.amazonaws.com/2016/03/29/13/44/01/e25c94e1-5a04-4e41-9fb0-2796d6abefdc/tipus-tiger2006AH4169.jpg\" class=\"bbcode-attachment bbcode-attachment--lightbox js-lightbox\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" itemprop=\"image\" loading=\"lazy\"/></div></div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> I was browsing through Amazon the other day and saw a review about an urban fantasy book that claimed it was good, but didn't portray the 'true events' of the magickal battle of Britain, which according to them involved <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dion_Fortune\" target=\"_blank\">Dion Fortune</a> and was detailed in a book called the Magical Battle for Britain.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":886,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","user":"nofather","date":". 12-10-2017, 04:07 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}